201 — Branching & Merging
Master Git branches to work on features independently and merge changes back together. Learn the branching workflow used by professional developers.

Step 1 Create a workspace for this lesson
Set up a fresh directory with an initialized Git repository and some initial commits to work with.
Commands to Run
mkdir -p ~/git-practice/lesson-201
cd ~/git-practice/lesson-201
git init
What This Does
We're creating a new workspace for this lesson to keep things organized. Starting fresh helps you focus on branching concepts without confusion from previous work.

Expected Outcome
You should see "Initialized empty Git repository" message and be in the lesson-201 directory

Step 2 Create initial content
Add a few files and commits to establish a baseline project. Branches need something to branch from!

Commands to Run
echo "# My Project" > README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "Initial commit: Add README"
echo "console.log('Hello, World!');" > app.js
git add app.js
git commit -m "Add main application file"
What This Does
These commits create a main branch with some content. In real projects, your main branch contains your stable, production-ready code.

Expected Outcome
Running `git log --oneline` should show two commits. Your main branch is now established.

Step 3 View existing branches
Learn to see what branches exist in your repository and which one you're currently on.

Commands to Run
git branch
What This Does
`git branch` lists all local branches. The branch with an asterisk (*) is your current branch. Right now, you only have the main branch.

Expected Outcome
You should see `* main` (or `* master`), indicating you're on the main branch.

Step 4 Create your first feature branch
Create a new branch to work on a feature independently from the main branch.

Commands to Run (git branch feature-login)

What This Does
Branches are pointers to commits. Creating a branch doesn't change your working directory - it just creates a new pointer. You're still on the main branch until you switch.

Step 5 Switch to the new branch
Move to your feature branch so changes you make won't affect the main branch.

Commands to Run (git checkout feature-login)

What This Does
`git checkout` switches your working directory to the specified branch. Now any commits you make will be on the feature-login branch, not main.

Step 6 Make commits on the feature branch
Add a login feature on your branch. These changes won't affect the main branch.

Commands to Run
echo "function login(user, pass) { /* TODO */ }" > login.js
git add login.js
git commit -m "Add login function skeleton"
echo "function logout() { /* TODO */ }" >> login.js
git add login.js
git commit -m "Add logout function"

What This Does
These commits exist only on the feature-login branch. The main branch is unchanged and still has only two commits. This isolation lets you experiment safely.

Step 7 Compare branches
See the differences between your feature branch and the main branch.

Commands to Run
git log --oneline --all --graph
ls

What This Does
The `--all --graph` flags show all branches and their relationships visually. Notice how the branches have diverged. The `ls` command shows login.js exists here.

Expected Outcome
You should see a visual graph showing both branches. You'll see login.js in the file list.

Step 8 Switch back to main branch
Return to the main branch to see that your feature changes aren't there yet.

Commands to Run
git checkout main
ls
git log --oneline
What This Does
When you switch branches, your working directory changes to reflect that branch's content. Notice that login.js disappears - it only exists on the feature branch.

Expected Outcome
login.js is not in the file list, and `git log` shows only 2 commits. Your feature work is safely stored in the feature-login branch.

Pro Tips
1. Files in your working directory change when you switch branches
2. Uncommitted changes can prevent branch switching - commit or stash first

Step 9 Merge the feature branch
Bring your feature work into the main branch by merging.

Commands to Run
git merge feature-login
git log --oneline --graph

What This Does
`git merge` combines the histories of two branches. Since main had no new commits while you worked on the feature, Git does a 'fast-forward' merge - it just moves the main pointer forward.

Expected Outcome
You should see "Fast-forward" and the log now shows all 4 commits on main. login.js now appears in `ls`.

Pro Tips
1. Always merge INTO the branch you want to update (checkout main, then merge feature)
2. Fast-forward merges are the simplest type

Step 10
Delete the feature branch
Clean up by removing the feature branch now that it's been merged.

Commands to Run (git branch -d feature-login)

What This Does
After merging, the feature branch is no longer needed. The `-d` flag safely deletes branches that have been merged. Your commits are still in the history.

Expected Outcome
You should see "Deleted branch feature-login" and only main remains in `git branch`.

Pro Tips
1. Use `-d` for safe deletion (only works if merged)
2. Use `-D` to force-delete an unmerged branch (use carefully!)

Step 11 Practice: Non-fast-forward merge
Create a scenario where both branches have new commits, requiring a merge commit.

Commands to Run
git checkout -b feature-signup
echo "function signup() { /* TODO */ }" > signup.js
git add signup.js
git commit -m "Add signup feature"
git checkout main
echo "## Installation" >> README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "Update README with installation steps"

What This Does
Now both branches have moved forward independently. This creates a divergent history that requires a real merge commit.

Expected Outcome
Running `git log --oneline --all --graph` should show the branches have diverged with different commits.

Pro Tips
1.This is the typical workflow in team environments
2.Both branches have valid work that needs to be combined

Step 13 Create a merge conflict (intentionally)
Learn to handle merge conflicts by creating one on purpose.

Commands to Run
git checkout -b feature-modify-app
echo "console.log('Starting app...');" > app.js
git add app.js
git commit -m "Add startup message"
git checkout main
echo "console.log('Application initialized');" > app.js
git add app.js
git commit -m "Add initialization message"

What This Does
Both branches modified app.js differently. When we try to merge, Git won't know which version to keep - this creates a conflict that requires human decision.

Expected Outcome
Both commits succeed. The branches now have conflicting changes to the same file.

Pro Tips
1 Conflicts happen when the same lines of a file are changed in different branches
2 Conflicts are normal in team development - don't panic!

Step 14 Attempt the merge and see the conflict
Try to merge the conflicting branch and observe what happens.

Commands to Run
git merge feature-modify-app
git status

What This Does
Git detects the conflict and stops the merge. The file is left in a special state with conflict markers showing both versions. You must resolve it manually.

Expected Outcome
You should see "CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in app.js" and "Automatic merge failed". `git status` shows app.js as "both modified".

Pro Tips
1. Don't panic - conflicts are easy to fix
2. Git marks the conflicting sections in the file with special markers

Step 15 View the conflict markers
Look at the conflicted file to understand how Git marked the conflict.

Commands to Run
cat app.js ( in local type app.js )

What This Does
Git inserts conflict markers into the file: `<<<<<<< HEAD` (your current branch), `=======` (separator), and `>>>>>>> feature-modify-app` (the incoming branch). You decide what to keep.

Expected Outcome
You should see the conflict markers with both versions of the code between them.

Pro Tips
1 Everything between <<<< and ==== is from your current branch
2 Everything between ==== and >>>> is from the branch you're merging
3 You can keep one, both, or write something entirely new

Step 16 Resolve the conflict
Edit the file to resolve the conflict by choosing what to keep.

Commands to Run
echo "console.log('Application initialized and starting...');" > app.js
cat app.js

What This Does
We've resolved the conflict by creating a new version that combines the intent of both changes. In practice, you'd use a text editor to carefully merge the changes.

Expected Outcome
app.js now contains only your resolved version, with no conflict markers.

Pro Tips
1 Remove ALL conflict markers (<<<<<, =====, >>>>>)
2 Make sure the final code makes sense and works
3 You can test the code before committing

Step 17 Complete the merge
Stage the resolved file and commit to finish the merge.

Commands to Run
git add app.js
git status
git commit -m "Merge feature-modify-app and resolve conflict"
git log --oneline --graph

What This Does
After resolving conflicts and staging the file, commit to complete the merge. Git knows you're in the middle of a merge and creates a merge commit.

Expected Outcome
The merge is complete. `git status` shows a clean working tree, and the log shows the merge commit.

Pro Tips
1 Always run `git status` to ensure all conflicts are resolved
2 The commit message should mention what was merged and how conflicts were resolved

Step 18 Clean up
Delete the merged feature branches to keep your branch list clean.

Commands to Run
git branch -d feature-signup feature-modify-app
git branch

What This Does
After merging, feature branches are usually deleted. The work is preserved in the commit history. Keeping the branch list clean makes it easier to see what's in progress.

Expected Outcome
Both feature branches are deleted. Only main remains.

Pro Tips
1 You can delete multiple branches in one command
2 Regularly clean up merged branches

